Update the Kubernetes Log Streamer based on the following requirements.

1. Analyze the sample log files

Before changing the parser, inspect all log files in the provided sample-log folder.

Identify:
- Distinct log formats/patterns
- Timestamp formats and locations
- Log levels
- Log entry boundaries
- Multiline/stack-trace patterns
- Embedded JSON or structured content
- Common patterns across applications/namespaces

Do not create application-specific or namespace-specific parsers. The parser must detect the format from the log structure at runtime.

Also inspect the existing Pega Log Streamer implementation and reuse its existing parsing/rendering/search behavior where applicable.

2. Static logs only

Remove live streaming functionality from Kubernetes Log Streamer:
- Remove Go Live
- Remove Stop Live
- Remove WebSocket/live-stream handling
- Remove Terminate Session

The Kubernetes Log Streamer should only retrieve static logs.

3. Refresh

Replace Reload Session with Refresh.

Refresh should:
- Fetch the latest static logs
- Show a loading state while fetching
- Parse the response using the generic parser
- Merge new entries with currently loaded entries
- Deduplicate existing entries
- Automatically move the view to the newest log entry after refresh

Do not recreate a live session.

4. Initial log loading

Fetch a maximum of 500 raw lines initially.

Pipeline:

raw Kubernetes logs → format detection → parsing → multiline grouping → structured log entries → rendering

Do not limit the result to 500 rendered entries because multiline entries may contain multiple raw lines.

5. Older logs

When the user scrolls to the top of the currently loaded logs, automatically fetch the next older chunk of logs.

Use 500 raw lines per fetch unless the existing backend/API requires a different practical value.

When older entries are inserted, preserve the user's current viewport position so the screen does not jump.

6. Initial position

After the initial load, position the viewer at the newest log entry at the bottom.

Latest logs must appear at the bottom and older logs above them.

7. Timestamp

Do not display the fetch/request timestamp as the log timestamp.

Parse and display the actual timestamp contained in each log entry.

Each log entry must retain its own timestamp.

8. Log parsing/rendering

Do not flatten structured logs into unnecessary columns.

Parse the raw log into structured entries containing the relevant metadata and message, then render them in the same style as the existing Pega Log Streamer where applicable.

Support the identified organizational log formats generically.

Multiline entries must be grouped into a single log entry.

9. Multiline logs

Group stack traces and continuation lines with their parent log entry.

Display a collapsed preview of approximately 4 lines by default.

Allow the user to expand/collapse the complete multiline entry.

10. Search/filter

Reuse the existing Pega Log Streamer search behavior where possible.

Search should:
- Filter matching log entries
- Highlight the exact matching text
- Support existing regex and case-sensitive options
- Keep the existing filter UI behavior

11. Remove unnecessary UI

Remove:
- Go Live
- Stop Live
- Terminate Session
- Previous Container Logs
- The green informational banner about static/live mode

Keep Back to Discovery.

Back to Discovery must clean up the current viewer state/session resources and return to the pod discovery table.

12. UI consistency

Use the existing Pega Log Streamer UI as the reference for:
- Log entry layout
- Search/filter behavior
- Controls
- Styling
- Spacing
- Pills/buttons
- Loading states
- Scrolling behavior

Do not introduce a separate visual design for Kubernetes logs.

13. Validation

After implementation, test end-to-end with the provided sample logs and verify:

- All sample formats can be detected and parsed
- Actual timestamps are displayed
- Multiline entries are grouped correctly
- Initial load retrieves only 500 raw lines
- Viewer opens at the newest entry
- Older logs load when scrolling to the top
- Refresh retrieves and appends new entries without duplication
- Refresh moves to the newest entry
- Search highlighting works
- Back to Discovery cleans up the viewer state
- No live-stream/WebSocket functionality remains
- Existing Pega Log Streamer behavior is not broken

Do not implement Kibana-style analytics/metrics in this change. That will be handled separately.
